1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:01,550
In 2018,

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:06,289
Kīlauea's summit collapsed as its magma
drained away to the lower East Rift Zone.

3
00:00:07,099 --> 00:00:09,489
Kīlauea is on the Island of Hawaiʻi.

4
00:00:09,689 --> 00:00:13,249
This summary follows
a two-page report by the U.S.

5
00:00:13,249 --> 00:00:14,766
Geological Survey.

6
00:00:15,596 --> 00:00:16,586
On April 30,

7
00:00:16,586 --> 00:00:18,526
the Puʻu ʻŌʻō vent collapsed.

8
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:20,946
Fissure eruptions began on May 3,

9
00:00:20,946 --> 00:00:24,521
and the next day a magnitude
6.9 earthquake struck.

10
00:00:25,331 --> 00:00:27,181
Meanwhile, the summit sank.

11
00:00:27,401 --> 00:00:28,301
By mid-May,

12
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:32,142
shaking and ground tilt had forced
the Hawaiian Volcano Observatory out of

13
00:00:32,142 --> 00:00:32,991
its building.

14
00:00:33,261 --> 00:00:34,551
From May 29,

15
00:00:34,551 --> 00:00:36,641
the summit collapsed almost daily,

16
00:00:36,691 --> 00:00:39,684
each with the energy
of a magnitude 5 earthquake.

17
00:00:40,504 --> 00:00:41,384
Down the rift,

18
00:00:41,384 --> 00:00:44,415
fissure 8's fountains
rose 200 feet at times.

19
00:00:45,345 --> 00:00:49,352
Its river of lava reached
the ocean at Kapoho Bay on June 3.

20
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:53,580
Lava covered 13.7 square miles.

21
00:00:53,765 --> 00:00:57,520
Hawaiʻi County counted
716 homes destroyed.

22
00:00:57,749 --> 00:01:01,460
Ocean entries built 875 acres of new land,

23
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:04,290
and about 60,000
earthquakes shook the area.

24
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:07,460
In early August, the eruption faded.

25
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:09,110
On September 22,

26
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:11,195
the national park partly reopened.
